    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p25-q4a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Use mathematical induction to prove that $n^2+n$ is divisible by $2$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$2\\mid(n^2+n)$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.\n\nThe base case is $2$; the inductive step gives $2(m+k+1)$.",
          "worked": "Base case: for $n=1$, $1^2+1=2$, which is divisible by $2$.\n\nInductive hypothesis: assume $k^2+k=2m$ for some integer $m$ and $k\\ge1$.\n\nInductive step:\n$$\\begin{align*}(k+1)^2+(k+1)&=k^2+2k+1+k+1\\\\(k+1)^2+(k+1)&=(k^2+k)+2k+2\\\\(k+1)^2+(k+1)&=2m+2k+2\\\\(k+1)^2+(k+1)&=2(m+k+1).\\end{align*}$$\nThis is divisible by $2$. By mathematical induction, $n^2+n$ is divisible by $2$ for all $n\\in\\mathbb N$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p25-q4b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Can you justify this result without using mathematical induction?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$n^2+n=n(n+1)$; one of these consecutive integers is even.",
          "worked": "$$n^2+n=n(n+1).$$\nThe factors are consecutive integers, so one is even. Their product is therefore divisible by $2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      }
    ]
  },
